# SDD §5 — Secure Boot policy. See docs/adr/0002-secure-boot.md.
#
# Debian ships a signed shim and a signed GRUB, but no signed systemd-boot.
# ik-os therefore signs systemd-boot with a company Machine Owner Key that is
# enrolled once per machine (or shipped in the firmware db by IT).
#
#   required — the build fails unless a signing key is provided. Use this for
#              the testing and stable channels.
#   optional — an unsigned systemd-boot is produced. Local developer builds only.
SECURE_BOOT_SIGNING=optional

# The key is secret and the certificate is not, so they live in different places
# — the same split as cosign (config/company/cosign.pub committed, the private
# half only in the SIGNING_SECRET secret).
#
# The certificate is committed: it is public by construction, it ships in every
# signed image at /usr/share/ik-os/ik-os-mok.crt, and having it in the tree lets
# the build assert that the key it was handed still matches the certificate the
# fleet has enrolled. A MOK swapped by accident would otherwise produce images
# that simply do not boot on every machine already enrolled.
SECURE_BOOT_CERT="${CTX:-/ctx}/config/company/ik-os-mok.crt"

# The key is written here from the SECURE_BOOT_MOK_KEY secret by build.yml. For a
# local signed build, point this at a key you hold and leave the line above
# alone; `sbsign` needs an unencrypted key, because nothing can type a
# passphrase for it.
SECURE_BOOT_KEY="${CTX:-/ctx}/secrets/ik-os-mok.key"
